1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,141
Beach sand burns your feet by
noon, but the sea stays cool.

2
00:00:04,141 --> 00:00:08,648
A steel pan gets hot in a minute, but
the water in it takes much longer.

3
00:00:09,048 --> 00:00:10,596
Same sun, same flame.

4
00:00:10,596 --> 00:00:12,485
So why such different speeds?

5
00:00:12,935 --> 00:00:14,984
Diya puts a pan of water on the stove.

6
00:00:15,334 --> 00:00:18,614
In a minute, the rim of the
pan is too hot to touch.

7
00:00:18,614 --> 00:00:20,382
The water is only just warm.

8
00:00:20,882 --> 00:00:23,020
By the end, you will know why.

9
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:25,732
It is all about enthalpy
and heat capacity.

10
00:00:26,382 --> 00:00:29,830
Last time we learnt,
delta U equals q plus w.

11
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:33,494
In a sealed steel box,
the volume cannot change.

12
00:00:33,494 --> 00:00:37,351
So the gas does no work, and
all the heat goes into U.

13
00:00:37,751 --> 00:00:41,914
But in a kitchen, the pressure stays
steady and the volume can change.

14
00:00:42,314 --> 00:00:46,770
A gas that forms must push the air
away, like making room in a crowded

15
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:47,670
bus.

16
00:00:47,269 --> 00:00:48,875
That push costs energy.

17
00:00:49,275 --> 00:00:53,065
So chemists use a new state
function, enthalpy, H.

18
00:00:53,065 --> 00:00:54,813
H equals U plus p V.

19
00:00:55,213 --> 00:00:58,926
At constant pressure, the
heat taken in equals delta H.

20
00:00:59,576 --> 00:01:03,839
Now the link between delta
H and delta U, step by step.

21
00:01:04,189 --> 00:01:08,799
At constant pressure, delta H
equals delta U plus p delta V.

22
00:01:09,549 --> 00:01:12,244
For gases, p V equals n RT.

23
00:01:12,244 --> 00:01:15,325
So p delta V equals delta n g RT.

24
00:01:16,075 --> 00:01:20,202
Delta n g is the moles of gas in the
products, minus the moles of gas in

25
00:01:20,202 --> 00:01:21,128
the reactants.

26
00:01:21,878 --> 00:01:25,683
So delta H equals delta
U plus delta n g RT.

27
00:01:26,433 --> 00:01:29,154
If delta H is minus, heat comes out.

28
00:01:29,154 --> 00:01:30,581
That is exothermic.

29
00:01:30,581 --> 00:01:33,147
If delta H is plus, heat goes in.

30
00:01:33,147 --> 00:01:34,627
That is endothermic.

31
00:01:35,277 --> 00:01:38,976
Now, back to the pan and the
water, on our molecule bench.

32
00:01:39,326 --> 00:01:44,235
Heat capacity, C, is the heat needed
to warm something by one degree.

33
00:01:44,235 --> 00:01:46,186
q equals C times delta T.

34
00:01:46,586 --> 00:01:49,289
A big heat capacity is like a wide bucket.

35
00:01:49,289 --> 00:01:52,421
Pour in lots, and the
level rises only a little.

36
00:01:52,821 --> 00:01:57,156
Specific heat capacity is the
heat for one gram, by one kelvin.

37
00:01:57,156 --> 00:02:00,321
For water, about four
point one eight joules.

38
00:02:00,721 --> 00:02:02,766
So q equals m c delta T.

39
00:02:02,766 --> 00:02:06,014
Mass, times specific heat, times the rise.

40
00:02:06,414 --> 00:02:11,022
To warm one hundred grams of water
by ten kelvin, you need four thousand

41
00:02:11,022 --> 00:02:12,819
one hundred eighty joules.

42
00:02:13,219 --> 00:02:15,638
Steel needs far less heat per gram.

43
00:02:15,638 --> 00:02:19,087
So the pan races ahead,
and the water lags behind.

44
00:02:19,087 --> 00:02:21,306
Sand and sea work the same way.

45
00:02:21,956 --> 00:02:25,495
For one mole of an ideal gas,
there are two heat capacities.

46
00:02:25,845 --> 00:02:27,808
C v, at constant volume.

47
00:02:27,808 --> 00:02:29,887
All the heat warms the gas.

48
00:02:30,287 --> 00:02:32,235
C p, at constant pressure.

49
00:02:32,235 --> 00:02:35,013
Some heat is spent pushing the piston up.

50
00:02:35,013 --> 00:02:36,411
So C p is bigger.

51
00:02:36,811 --> 00:02:38,745
The gap is exactly R.

52
00:02:38,745 --> 00:02:40,946
C p minus C v equals R.

53
00:02:41,596 --> 00:02:44,145
We measure these heats with a calorimeter.

54
00:02:44,145 --> 00:02:47,366
A bomb calorimeter is a
sealed steel vessel in water.

55
00:02:47,366 --> 00:02:48,738
Its volume is fixed.

56
00:02:49,138 --> 00:02:53,551
A coffee cup calorimeter is open
to the air, at steady pressure.

57
00:02:53,951 --> 00:02:59,000
In both, we read how much the water
warms, and use q equals C delta T.

58
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:00,555
Pause and think.

59
00:03:00,555 --> 00:03:03,352
A reaction runs in a
sealed bomb calorimeter.

60
00:03:03,352 --> 00:03:05,539
Does it give delta U or delta H?

61
00:03:08,739 --> 00:03:09,639
Delta U!

62
00:03:09,548 --> 00:03:14,424
The volume cannot change, so no work
is done, and the heat equals delta

63
00:03:14,424 --> 00:03:15,324
U.

64
00:03:15,504 --> 00:03:16,630
Remember this.

65
00:03:16,630 --> 00:03:18,787
Constant volume gives delta U.

66
00:03:18,787 --> 00:03:21,049
Constant pressure gives delta H.

67
00:03:21,749 --> 00:03:23,993
Now, one worked example, in NCERT style.

68
00:03:24,293 --> 00:03:27,852
Water boils at one bar and
three seventy three kelvin.

69
00:03:27,852 --> 00:03:30,899
Delta H for one mole is
forty one kilojoules.

70
00:03:30,899 --> 00:03:31,981
Find delta U.

71
00:03:32,381 --> 00:03:35,018
Liquid water turns into water vapour.

72
00:03:35,018 --> 00:03:36,786
One mole of gas forms.

73
00:03:36,786 --> 00:03:38,768
The liquid counts as zero.

74
00:03:39,518 --> 00:03:43,367
So delta n g is one
minus zero, which is one.

75
00:03:44,117 --> 00:03:47,860
Delta U equals delta H minus delta n g RT.

76
00:03:48,610 --> 00:03:53,154
RT is eight point three one four times
ten to the minus three, times three

77
00:03:53,154 --> 00:03:54,155
seventy three.

78
00:03:54,155 --> 00:03:56,212
About three point one kilojoules.

79
00:03:56,962 --> 00:03:59,638
Delta U is forty one
minus three point one.

80
00:03:59,638 --> 00:04:02,577
About thirty seven point
nine kilojoules per mole.

81
00:04:03,227 --> 00:04:04,689
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:04:05,039 --> 00:04:07,259
Many students count liquids
and solids in delta n g.

83
00:04:07,659 --> 00:04:08,559
No.

84
00:04:08,035 --> 00:04:09,296
Only gas moles count.

85
00:04:09,296 --> 00:04:11,645
Liquids and solids
barely change the volume.

86
00:04:12,295 --> 00:04:13,665
Where is this asked?

87
00:04:13,665 --> 00:04:17,933
Expect numericals linking delta H and
delta U, and questions on C p minus

88
00:04:17,933 --> 00:04:18,833
C v.

89
00:04:19,057 --> 00:04:20,240
Exam level now.

90
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:23,269
Nitrogen plus three
hydrogen gives two ammonia.

91
00:04:23,269 --> 00:04:27,711
Delta H is minus ninety two point
four kilojoules, at two ninety eight

92
00:04:27,711 --> 00:04:28,611
kelvin.

93
00:04:28,373 --> 00:04:29,456
Find delta U.

94
00:04:29,456 --> 00:04:30,689
Pause and solve.

95
00:04:36,089 --> 00:04:37,425
The answer is A.

96
00:04:37,425 --> 00:04:39,547
Minus eighty seven point four.

97
00:04:40,047 --> 00:04:42,215
Gas moles: two minus four.

98
00:04:42,215 --> 00:04:44,172
Delta n g is minus two.

99
00:04:44,672 --> 00:04:49,482
Delta n g RT is minus two, times
eight point three one four times ten

100
00:04:49,482 --> 00:04:52,590
to the minus three,
times two ninety eight.

101
00:04:52,590 --> 00:04:54,573
Minus four point nine five.

102
00:04:55,073 --> 00:04:59,698
Delta U is minus ninety two point
four, plus four point nine five.

103
00:04:59,698 --> 00:05:01,839
Minus eighty seven point four.

104
00:05:01,839 --> 00:05:05,138
C is the trap, from
delta n g of minus one.

105
00:05:05,738 --> 00:05:07,279
Here is your recap.

106
00:05:07,279 --> 00:05:08,863
Screenshot this card.

107
00:05:12,063 --> 00:05:14,249
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

108
00:05:14,649 --> 00:05:15,549
One.

109
00:05:15,176 --> 00:05:18,692
Does a bomb calorimeter
measure delta U or delta H?

110
00:05:19,492 --> 00:05:20,392
Two.

111
00:05:19,923 --> 00:05:21,885
Why does water warm up so slowly?

112
00:05:22,685 --> 00:05:23,585
Three.

113
00:05:23,299 --> 00:05:27,115
What is C p minus C v, for
one mole of an ideal gas?

114
00:05:28,015 --> 00:05:32,129
Next time: how much heat does one
mole of cooking gas give out when it

115
00:05:32,129 --> 00:05:33,029
burns?

116
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:35,375
We find out in Enthalpy
Changes of Reactions.
